ation of safety policy\n- NO modification of other project workspaces\n- NO systemd administration unless explicitly required by a tightly scoped service mechanism\n---\n## 5. Level 2A Definition Tighten করো\n`Single file` alone does NOT make an operation safe.\nLevel 2A requires ALL:\n- single file\n- approved project subtree\n- non-sensitive path\n- non-policy file\n- non-hook file\n- non-secret file\n- non-auth file\n- non-infrastructure file\n- non-dependency file\n- non-schema file\n- validated diff\n- required local test passes\nOtherwise → Level 2B or DENY.\n---\n## 6. Docker Must Be Operation-Aware\nDo not classify all Docker commands as Level 3.\nExamples:\n```text\ndocker ps\ndocker inspect\nmay be read-only.\nBut:\ndocker run -v /:/host\ndocker run --privileged\ndocker exec with host/root access\nmust be hard-denied.\nThe policy must inspect Docker arguments, not only the executable name.\n---\n7. Git Must Be Operation-Aware\nExamples:\ngit status\ngit diff\ngit log\nare read-only.\nPotentially allowed project-local operations may include:\ngit add\ngit commit\nsubject to policy.\nBut autonomous mode must hard-deny:\ngit push\ngit push --force\ngit reset --hard\ngit clean -fd\nunless a future explicit policy changes this.\n---\n8. Policy Self-Protection\nThe gateway MUST protect:\n~/.gemini/config/hooks.json\nAGY-MASTER/POLICIES/\npolicy_gateway.py\nsafety_levels.json\nAGY-MASTER/AGENTS.md\nglobal AGENTS.md\nfrom modification by ordinary project agents.\nDo not rely only on the gateway to protect itself.\nUse filesystem ownership/permissions and separate agent identity where possible.\n---\n9. No Blind Rule Duplication\nBefore modifying project \"AGENTS.md\" files:\nCreate a Rule Distribution Matrix:\nRule\nSource\nGlobal?\nMaster?\nProject?\nDuplicate?\nMissing?\nAuthoritative Location?\nDo not copy the entire 77KB \"AGENTS.md\".\nDo not modify rules merely because they appear in the audit.\nPreserve the existing authoritative meaning.\n---\n10. IMPLEMENTATION ORDER\nAfter the above gates are verified:\nPhase A\nCreate policy gateway + unit tests.\nNO hook activation yet.\nPhase B\nRun adversarial offline tests:\n- unknown agent\n- unknown tool\n- malformed input\n- missing policy\n- path traversal\n- symlink escape\n- sensitive path\n- sudo\n- docker privileged\n- rm -rf\n- git reset --hard\n- git push\n- database destructive operation\n- hook self-edit\n- policy self-edit\n- cross-project write\n- allowed project-local edit\nExpected rule:\n\"UNKNOWN = DENY\"\n\"ERROR = DENY\"\n\"INVALID = DENY\"\n\"UNSAFE = DENY\"\nPhase C\nOnly after all tests pass, register gateway in PreToolUse.\nPhase D\nRun live non-destructive verification.\nPhase E\nOnly then perform OS-level containment hardening.\nPhase F\nOnly then localize project-specific rules.\nPhase G\nOnly then implement concurrency/lease/atomic state improvements.\nABSOLUTE SAFETY RULE\nDo NOT make any production modification until the relevant phase's test evidence exists.\nDo NOT claim:\n\"100% safe\"\n\"OS-level protected\"\n\"fully machine enforced\"\nunless the corresponding mechanism has actually been tested.\nEvery final claim must use:\n\"Mechanism → Evidence → Test → Result → Limitation\"\nThe system must remain:\n\"APPROVAL-LESS\"\nbut never:\n\"POLICY-LESS\"\nProceed only according to this phased order.Bottom line: রিপোর্টের architecture direction এখন ভালো। কিন্তু আমি বিশেষভাবে OS identity separation + fail-closed timeout verification + tighter Level 2A—এই তিনটা না যোগ করে gateway implementation শুরু করতাম না।\nআর একটা ভালো খবর: Antigravity-এর বর্তমান official permission system-এ deny > ask > allow precedence আছে, তাই ভবিষ্যতে চাইলে native deny rules-ও দ্বিতীয় application-level safeguard হিসেবে রাখা যায়। কিন্তু --dangerously-skip-permissions থাকলে সেটাকে primary safety mechanism হিসেবে ধরে নেওয়া যাবে না।\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-25T08:01:16Z.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":5248,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T08:01:16Z","thinking":"Pre-tool use safeguards are insufficient; several system functionalities can potentially circumvent them. The architecture necessitates a four-layered structure to address this vulnerability.\n\n\nThe current security design is flawed due to shared identities and unchecked assumptions about timeout behavior, necessitating an immediate redesign. Implementing robust identity separation is crucial: separate identities for human administration, the master agent, and project-level agents, with worker agents having severely restricted privileges. Furthermore, comprehensive testing of the policy enforcement hook is required under various failure conditions to ensure fail-closed behavior on timeout. Granular policy application is required with particular attention to dangerous operations, particularly with Docker and Git.\n\n\nGit operations require careful policy enforcement, specifically categorizing commands by risk level, with \"hard deny\" for destructive actions. Security level 2A requires strict conditions beyond just single-file modifications to avoid dangerous edits. The policy system itself must be protected, and the distribution of security rules needs a structured approach. Verification of hook behavior under failure conditions is also crucial, and creation of the policy gateway, along with its unit tests, must happen before any hook activation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"ls -la /home/azureuser/.gemini/config/hooks.json 2>/dev/null || echo \"hooks.json does not exist\"","Cwd":"/home/azureuser","RunPersistent":true,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking hooks.json existence","toolSummary":"Check if hooks.json exists"}}]}
